{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And if you shall say: What shall we eat in the seventh year.</b> There are commentators who explain that 'in the seventh year' does not attach to 'and if you shall say,' but rather: And if you shall say in the sixth year, 'What shall we eat in the seventh year' — for in the seventh year they do not sow; but in the seventh year they would surely not say, 'What shall we eat in the eighth,' since it is already stated ״וְעָשָׂת אֶת הַתְּבוּאָה לִשְׁלֹשׁ הַשָּׁנִים״ — if so, they already saw the abundance of produce in the sixth year. And this is a difficulty upon the Ramban and Maharil, who explained thus, that they would say in the seventh year, 'What shall we eat in the eighth.' But according to what we wrote above, that this blessing is such that one eats a little and is satisfied in his innards, this is no difficulty, for after all they would not see an abundance of produce. And there are those who say that everyone who has a little bread in his hand immediately pinches and ration it at once, out of fear lest it be lacking for him in the future. So too they would pinch even in the seventh year, out of fear of the shortfall of produce in the eighth." "And it appears to me that the explanation is thus: this entire complaint revolves around what is stated in the preceding verse, ״וַאֲכַלְתֶּם לַחְמְכֶם לָשֹׂבַע״, and the Sages said (see Yoma 14a): It is written (Deuteronomy 8:16), ״הַמַּאֲכִלְךָ מָן בַּמִּדְבָּר״, and it is written (ibid. 8:3), ״וַיְעַנְּךָ וַיַּרְעִבֶךָ״? Rather, one who has bread in his basket is not comparable to one who does not. You see that even though the manna came down each day, nevertheless they would eat and be hungry, because there was no bread in their basket for the morrow. Therefore here too they would surely say ״מַה נֹּאכַל בַּשָּׁנָה הַשְּׁבִיעִית״ before they saw the abundance of produce in the sixth year, and they would say: If we were to sow in the seventh year for the needs of the eighth, we would have bread in the basket and we would eat and be satisfied; but since we did not sow for the needs of the eighth, if so we will not have bread in the basket, and if so even in the seventh year we will not be satisfied, and all the more so in the eighth — and where is the guarantee of Him who said ״וַאֲכַלְתֶּם לַחְמְכֶם לָשֹׂבַע״? And what shall we eat that will be satisfying, since we did not sow and did not gather our produce for the needs of the eighth? About this He said ״וְצִוִּיתִי אֶת בִּרְכָתִי״ etc., in order that you may have bread in the basket also for the needs of the eighth, and then they will eat their bread to satisfaction in the seventh year, for they will have bread in their basket for three years. And there are those who say that 'three years' is because of the Jubilee year, in which they do not sow for two consecutive years. And it is possible to explain 'three years' for every shemittah, and they would say so on account of the lack of the aftergrowth of the eighth year, even if they have of the old grain in hand. And in place of the aftergrowth He promised them that the produce would suffice for an extra year, to fill the lack of the aftergrowth, so that they would eat and be satisfied when they have produce in great abundance and not with meager rationing."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:35.213380967Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}